    "q-121345": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121345",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/96b70c61e20b693a29a125b5f03243a75d3bfd0aeb051a2f6f74525cfdb3da78.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and flies at a constant angle of ${45}^{∘}$ to the horizontal. How fast is the drone gaining altitude if its speed is $42\\,\\text{km/h}?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/40e0ae889d241c45464492ba3842d586effe528ab93b3554f6dbfb01d215c308.png)\n\nLet $y$ be the drone's altitude and $z$ be the actual distance between the drone and its take-off point, as shown below.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=42\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 42=21\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
