    "q-53196": {
      "dom_id": "step-q53196",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/d486581ea48bc96adc1269fbf40d1d71a547e92977f71e3a74336a147ef9b222.png)\n\nA potato $P$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians from the horizontal. The horizontal distance the projectile travels increases at a rate of $18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ If the angle of elevation does not change, what is the speed of the potato?",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/8b5af04f58fe65b46adaae47d97b857d1e8765122a811179cf25ba22733e6415.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the horizontal distance from the potato to the cannon and $z$ be the actual distance between them, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 18=12\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the potato is $12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
