    "q-121348": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121348",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/5e96676aca66b99fd1e080d6c0cbbe79a5197125a3bcc8f04e67ca406947af10.png)\n\nA bullet $B$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ from the horizontal. Given that the bullet's horizontal speed is $300\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ calculate the actual speed of the bullet in the direction it is traveling.",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/fea05a36487813579d4633d9134bdd1d8c4d4bef4e9f57ba997b7b8715d46f32.png)\n\nLet $x$ denote the horizontal distance from the bullet's projection to the cannon, and $z$ be the distance between the bullet and the cannon, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=300\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2\\cdot 300=600.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the plane is $600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
