    "q-83678": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83678",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/6a37ae024b0472dfc38ecff01b898d8d3ed2384d90acf432d747efee1568520e.png)\n\nA car $C$ drives toward a $108\\,\\text{m}$ high building $B$ following a straight path. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the car and the top of the building $T$ is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ radians per second. What is the speed of the car when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi (\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
