    "q-86335": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86335",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/ce513880934a99b289c22f734e8a455c3534bddd911968cd06b7fe88f65441aa.png)\n\nA hot air balloon $B$ is flying horizontally to the observer $O$ at a constant height of $300\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the balloon and an observer is increasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the balloon when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
