    "q-2910": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2910",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/a51d0d8ef1be2e7fc06aa34fe1440e77a79d8bee654703c1e931332390741c32.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ ladder leans against the side of a building, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. Given that the foot of the ladder $F$ is being pulled away from the building at the rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/s}$, at what rate is $\\theta $ decreasing at the moment when the top of the ladder $T$ is $12\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground?",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/7b923b76e90d665a722643684ce94fff50500a7be8b70aa821410a7969e4bb79.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the foot of the ladder to the wall of the building, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the ladder. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the ladder's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=12$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{26} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\cdot \\frac{13}{12}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\theta $ is *decreasing* at a rate of $\\frac{1}{24}$ radians per second.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
