    "q-343195": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343195",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/2242cf493c6307d8c6f2dac778efe6f89ca4ba6f04203940076360613c8dc9b0.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ support pole leans against a vertical wall, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. The base of the pole $B$ is being pulled toward the wall at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ At what rate is $\\theta $ increasing at the moment when the top of the pole $T$ is $5\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/e08cddcde0d709ebe455b8d8935174955798b877f054dab0de74d06035accc91.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the base of the pole to the wall, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the pole and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the pole. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the pole's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=5$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-2$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{13} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$",
